[['signals',['afl10','Simpson-Vlach & Ellis']],['compare',['tufte83','Tufte 1983']],['dense',['johansson08','Johansson']],['rhythm',['cmudict','CMU Pronouncing Dictionary']],['vocab',['plaven','Plavén-Sigray et al.']],['vocab',['conc14','Brysbaert, Warriner & Kuperman']],['vocab',['aoa12','Kuperman et al.']],['vocab',['subtlex09','Brysbaert & New']],['vocab',['prev19','Brysbaert et al.']]].forEach(([k,p])=>{SRC[k]=SRC[k]||[];if(!SRC[k].some(x=>Array.isArray(x)&&x[0]===p[0]))SRC[k].push(p);});

/* ---------- the guide: every lens on one page, built from the same definitions the lenses use ---------- */
const MOFF={clutter:'The rules above find spare words. Switch the model on to see pairs of sentences, standing apart in the draft, that are close enough in meaning to be one point made twice.',flow:'The ladder above joins repeated words. Switch the model on to see, by meaning, whether each sentence opens from where the one before closed, even when no word is repeated.',grid:'The rules above only see repeated words. Switch the model on to also see which sentences and paragraphs are close in meaning, including the ones that share no word.',outline:'Switch the model on to see which sentence in each paragraph is closest in meaning to the rest, and where the point you stated is carried.',tracer:'The tracer above follows the words of your claim. Switch the model on to see where the draft states the claim in other words, and which paragraphs are furthest from it.',source:'The rule above finds copied strings of words. Switch the model on to also find sentences that restate a source sentence in different words, and the parts of the source your draft says least about.',compare:'Switch the model on to see how far each rewritten sentence has moved in meaning from the one it replaced.',terms:'Switch the model on to see pairs of terms that are close in meaning and may be two names for one thing.',mviews:'These views are drawn from the meaning model, so they need it switched on.'};
const MDOC={clutter:'The pairs of sentences that are not neighbours and are closest in meaning, as candidates for a point made twice. The model scores a statement and its denial as close, so each pair needs reading. With the entailment model loaded as well, each pair is read both ways and labelled as saying the same, contradicting, or neither.',flow:'A ribbon picture of each paragraph. Every sentence is divided at its main verb into an opening and a close, and the opening is compared with the close and the opening of the sentence before: a ribbon where it picks up the last close, a dashed line where it keeps the last opening, an amber line where it reaches further back, and an open circle for a fresh start.',grid:'Sentences the rules label “no link” are relabelled “meaning only” when they are close in meaning to the sentence before. The line above each paragraph adds how close it is to the paragraph before, and marks the weakest join in the draft. A picture sets each sentence’s closeness to the one before against the level that three-quarters of all pairs in the draft fall below, so a sentence that steps away from its neighbour stands out.',outline:'Each paragraph shows which sentence is closest in meaning to the rest, whether that sentence sits at an edge, and which sentence is furthest from the rest. A strip of dots ranks every sentence of the draft by how far it sits below its own paragraph. If you typed the paragraph’s point, it shows which sentence carries that point best.',tracer:'It names the sentence that states your claim most directly, in your words or in others, and shows how close each paragraph is to the claim. In the Toulmin layout it finds the sentence nearest to a warrant, backing or rebuttal you type. With the entailment model loaded as well, every sentence close to the claim is read against it and those that read as contradicting it are listed, and each rebuttal is read against the sentence you named as its answer.',source:'A table of your sentences closest in meaning to a source sentence, including restatements that share no run of words, and the parts of the source your draft says least about. With the entailment model loaded as well, your closest sentences are read against the source sentences for contradiction.',compare:'A table of rewritten sentences with how close each is to the one it replaced, lowest first. It can show drift. It cannot confirm that a meaning was kept. With the entailment model loaded as well, each rewrite is read against the sentence it replaced: does the new one still follow from the old, and the old from the new.',terms:'The closest pairs in meaning among your terms, as candidates for two names for one thing. The model judges single words less well than sentences, so treat the list as a prompt.',mviews:'Eight views. Thread: closeness of each sentence to the one before, and to your claim. Quilt: every sentence against every other. Topics: the model’s grouping of sentences, drawn as the share of each topic in each sentence along the draft, since a sentence is seldom about one thing. Map: sentences as dots on a flat map made by principal component analysis, with dot size for length and colour for closeness to your claim. Reorder: move paragraphs and watch the joins. Ask: find the sentences closest to a question. Abstract & ending: which opening sentences the last paragraph takes up and what is new at the end, whether the ending returns to the opening, and what the abstract covers; the entailment model, when loaded, reads the ending against the opening sentences. Drills: two exercises built from your draft.'};
