const LIMITS={load:'Every strip is a count made by the rules of the other lenses, so it inherits their mistakes. The coloured bars are the tallest fifth of this draft, whatever its level: a draft of short, plain sentences still has a tallest fifth. Lexical density is counted without a part-of-speech tagger, so it is not comparable with published figures. The strips say where to look. They do not say the sentence is wrong.',sv:'The subject and the main verb are found by rules, not by a parser. A long or unusual sentence can be misread, so check which words are marked before acting on a note. The hardest case is a word that can be a noun or a verb (“results”, “support”, “report”). The rules settle it by what stands on either side, and they know about 600 verbs, so a verb outside that list with no ending to give it away (“ignore”, “force”) can be missed.',topic:'The lens sees what opens a sentence. It cannot tell whether that opening is the right topic for your argument.',stress:'It judges the close by familiar words, filler and citations. It cannot tell whether what you ended on is the point you meant to stress.',flow:'“Old” means a word used in an earlier sentence, matched by its stem. A synonym or a pronoun counts as new.',action:'Nominalisations are found by their endings and a fixed list. Some are missed, and some nouns that end like one are legitimate terms.',outline:'It shows first and last sentences and checks the words of the point you type. It does not judge whether the point is a good one.',jobs:'You supply the labels. The lens checks what you tell it against the usual order of moves, which a good paragraph may depart from.',tracer:'Claim words are matched by their stems, so a claim restated in other words is not seen unless the meaning model is on.',claims:'Strong wording is found from fixed lists. The lens cannot read your evidence. It compares the wording with the kind of evidence you name.',hedge:'Hedges are found from a fixed list. One hedge may be exactly right. The lens notices when several pile up.',grid:'Links are judged by repeated words, matched by stem. Two sentences can be tightly connected and share no word.',signals:'Moves are tagged from fixed cue phrases, so a move made in other words is not seen.',skeleton:'A way of reading, with no notes of its own.',rhythm:'The length bands are fixed. The rules for runs and for contrast between neighbours are this tool’s working values, not published norms.',dense:'Noun stacks and chains of prepositions are found by rules. A settled technical term can look like a stack, and so can a run of three nouns.',voice:'A passive is found from a form of “be” and a participle. A participle the rules do not know is missed. Adjectives that look like participles (“interested”, “related to”, “based on”) are set aside from a short list, so some are still counted as passives.',clutter:'Stock phrases come from a fixed list. A phrase on the list is sometimes the right one.',worn:'The lists are fixed and general. A word flagged as vague may be precise in your field.',vocab:'The two word lists, the New General Service List and the New Academic Word List, cover everyday English and the academic words that every field shares. Every field has ordinary terms outside them, so an unlisted word is not an error.',terms:'Terms are nouns found by rule and matched by stem. The lens cannot know that two different words name one thing until you tell it.',acr:'It recognises abbreviations by their capitals and looks for a definition nearby. An abbreviation defined in an unusual way may be reported as undefined.',people:'It applies general advice from a fixed list. Preferred terms differ between communities and change over time.',stats:'Only results reported in the usual form, such as t(28) = 2.20, p = .036, are rechecked. A result written another way is not checked. The check on means (GRIM) holds only for whole-number scores, such as counts or single rating items. Numbers are found by their form, so a year or a page number can be counted as a number.',checks:'Fixed patterns only. No mark does not mean no problem.',slips:'About two dozen patterns, chosen to be nearly always right. The rules have no dictionary, so they find no misspellings. Harper, a second checker that you can load in this lens, does check spelling and grammar. It lists places to look and makes no note, and it will query names and terms it does not know.',source:'It compares exact words. A close paraphrase is not seen unless the meaning model is on, and this is not a plagiarism check.',mviews:'Everything in this lens comes from the model, which does not see negation or logic, and whose scores only rank things inside one draft.',compare:'The receipt counts notes by rule. A rewrite counts only when it keeps at least four in ten of the sentence’s content words.',practice:'The drills have fixed answers for the built-in sentences. Drills built from your own draft use the lenses’ rules, with their limits.'};
const GNAME={1:['Gopen & Swan’s principles','The lenses built on the principles in Gopen and Swan’s article. Start here.'],2:['Is the argument clear?','The argument as a whole: what each paragraph is for, and how strongly each claim is worded.'],3:['Does it flow?','How sentences and paragraphs connect.'],4:['Is each sentence easy to read?','Single sentences: their length, their density, their voice, and the words to cut.'],5:['Are the words well chosen?','Single words: vague ones, rare ones, the names you give things, and the words you use for people.'],6:['Is it polished?','Proofreading: statistics, citations and figures, typing slips, and overlap with a source.'],7:['Your progress','What you have changed so far, and drills to practise on.']};
/* ---------- the Guide's examples: every lens's pictures, drawn by the page's own code from a fixed example draft ---------- */
const GEX=`# Introduction

Rent caps limit what landlords may charge sitting tenants. Supporters argue that caps keep tenants in their homes, and several studies agree (Smith, 2019; Lee & Park, 2020; Okafor et al., 2021). Critics reply that caps shrink the supply of rental housing (Diamond, 2019). However, little is known about how tenants themselves respond once a cap is lifted. Few studies have examined tenant mobility after decontrol. This paper aims to measure how often tenants move when a rent cap ends.

# Methods

We interviewed 48 tenants in three cities. Each tenant was asked about every move since 2015. Following Smith (2019), we coded a move as forced when the tenant named rent as the reason. The transcripts were coded twice by separate coders. We used a fixed-effects model to compare capped and uncapped flats (Angrist & Pischke, 2009).

Glaciers in the region have retreated since 1990. Meltwater feeds the reservoirs that glaciers once filled. Glaciers and meltwater shape the reservoirs each spring.

# Results

Tenants in capped flats moved far less often than tenants in uncapped flats. We found that forced moves doubled in the year after the cap was lifted. The results show that older tenants were the most likely to move. Rents rose by a fifth in the same year, as Diamond (2019) predicted.

# Discussion

These findings suggest that a rent cap protects tenant stability more than supply. This is consistent with Smith (2019) and with Lee and Park (2020), but not with Diamond (2019). One limitation is that the sample is small and drawn from three cities. Some landlords may also have sold their flats, which our interviews with tenants could not capture. Future work should follow landlords as well as tenants. Policy on rent caps should therefore weigh tenant mobility alongside housing supply.`,GEX_CLAIM='A rent cap protects tenant stability';
